<!--
    kuna infrastructure test: DWARF variant-part import (`option dwarfvariants`).

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 program-prep, external-refinement (the DWARF importer).
    P0 assertion:      option dwarfvariants on|off  (default on).

    A Rust tagged enum carries NO `DW_TAG_member` of its own: its whole layout
    lives under a `DW_TAG_variant_part`, whose `DW_AT_discr` names the
    discriminant member and whose `DW_TAG_variant` children carry a
    `DW_AT_discr_value` plus the variant's named payload struct. The sibling
    `dwarfstructs` import (DIV-86) walks `DW_TAG_member` children and therefore
    recovered a Rust enum's `DW_AT_byte_size` and ZERO fields.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture. Both passes clear it
    and load the same real x86-64 ELF fixture
    (`decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/dwarfvariants_x86_64`,
    `rustc 1.90.0 -C debuginfo=2 -C opt-level=1`, source vendored beside it),
    because a `bytechunk` carries no DWARF at all and this decision is made
    inside `load file`. The gate is read at load time, so the `option` line comes
    BEFORE the `load file` in each pass.

    Pass 1 (`option dwarfvariants off`) reproduces the bug:
      * `ret_result` is `fn(u32) -> Result<u32,u32>`, 8 bytes. A field-less
        aggregate has no width the ABI classifier can place, so it is treated as
        a hidden-return-buffer call: a phantom `rethidden` first parameter
        appears, the real parameter shifts one slot right, and the body then
        reads `x` out of the phantom.
      * `ret_three` is a 16-byte three-variant enum. That IS a real sret, so the
        parameters do not move, and what is lost is the layout: the discriminant
        and each variant's payload are written as raw byte offsets
        (`field_0x4`, `field_0x8`).

    Pass 2 (the shipped default, `option dwarfvariants on`) reproduces the fix:
      the discriminant is a named `tag` field, each variant's payload is a named
      union facet carrying the SOURCE variant name (`A`, `B`), and `ret_result`
      recovers the signature its source declared.

    The NEGATIVE: `ret_pair` returns a plain C-shaped `struct Pair { u32 lo, hi; }`
    from the same image. No C aggregate carries a variant part, so it must render
    IDENTICALLY in both passes; `ret_plain` is the same for a FIELDLESS enum,
    which rustc emits as a `DW_TAG_enumeration_type` that this pass never sees.

    Passes 3 and 4 load the second fixture, `dwarfvariants_overlay_x86_64`, and
    cover the NAMING RULE rather than the shapes. A union member selects itself by
    OFFSET and the discriminant is never consulted, so where two variants claim
    one byte range the facet the union scorer picks is not evidence of anything.
    `Result` is that case: `Ok.__0` and `Err.__0` are both at offset 8, always.
    So `put_res` must write the payload through the offset spelling and name
    NEITHER variant, while `put_opt`, whose `Option` has exactly one
    payload-carrying variant, must still name `Some`. Both keep the recovered
    `tag` field, and `r16` keeps the signature the import bought.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
